= Public-key cryptography =

== Overview ==
'''Public-key cryptography''' uses related public and private keys to perform complementary cryptographic operations without requiring every interacting party to share the same secret key in advance.

Depending on the scheme, a public key can be used to verify signatures, encrypt data or keys, or participate in key agreement, while the corresponding private key must remain controlled by its holder.

Public-key cryptography is a broad family, not one algorithm. RSA, elliptic-curve systems, and post-quantum schemes use different mathematical assumptions and support different operations.

A public key being public does not authenticate who controls the corresponding private key. Certificates, trusted directories, decentralized identifiers, or other binding mechanisms may be needed.

== Boundaries ==
* public-key cryptography ≠ encryption only
* public key ≠ identity proof by itself
* public-key cryptography ≠ blockchain

